# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: the Diamond Sutra 868, Bi Sheng's type 1039 to 1048, Jikji
# 1377, the Gutenberg Bible 1452 to 1455, Caxton's press in Westminster 1476.
lesson history-books-copied-and-printed v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=3 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Books copied by hand and printed"
  goal "Put the steps of printing in order from East Asia to Europe, and say what printing changed for readers."

  look {
    scene 26x11 {
      row all at=canvas(0, 0) space=1 align=end {
        printingpress press printed=1
        library lib
      }
    }
    say """
      For most of history every book was copied by hand, a page at a time. The oldest printed book
      with a date, the Diamond Sutra, was printed in China in 868 from carved wooden blocks. Between
      1039 and 1048 Bi Sheng made movable letters of baked clay, and in Korea the book Jikji was
      printed with metal letters in 1377. In Europe Gutenberg printed his Bible in the 1450s, and
      Caxton brought a press to England in 1476.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.scribe-days k=0
    }
    show history.scribe-days k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.print-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.print-between k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.writing-order k=4 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.scribe-days k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.print-order k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.print-between k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.castle-century k=4 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    level hard {
      show history.print-between k=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.why-print k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.scribes-to-match k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Printing began in East Asia and reached Europe in the 1450s. It made books many, cheap and the same every time."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that printing was invented in East Asia centuries before Europe, and that it
    changed who could own and read a book. The way in works out how long a scribe took to copy a
    book; the core orders the steps of printing, counts the years between them, and reviews the
    order of writing and a castle's century. The wrong idea to watch for is "Gutenberg invented
    printing": he made a press with metal letters for European alphabets, long after printing in
    China and Korea. The numbers for scribes and presses are round ones made up for the sums,
    and the lesson says so. If the child is stuck, print a potato stamp and count how quickly
    copies come. Off the page, find the oldest book in your home and where and when it was printed.
    England's key stage 2 history allows a theme that "extends pupils' chronological knowledge
    beyond 1066"; Russia's history programme for grade six names «Изобретение европейского
    книгопечатания; И. Гутенберг». China's 道德与法治 for grade five has 「古代科技 耀我中华」, with
    printing among the great inventions.
    Japan's 社会 takes up history only through Japan's own story, in grade six
    (「我が国の歴史上の主な事象について，人物の働きや代表的な文化遺産を中心に」, the course of study as read in its 2008 edition), so
    this lesson has no line of its own there; its timeline and source skills serve that grade.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example divides a short book by pages a day, the steps of printing come nearly in order, and the years counted are between Jikji and Caxton."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the books are long and the rates uneven, the steps are fully muddled, the years counted run from paper to printing, and the three-star scribes rest one day in six."
  }
}
